1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,850
GateReady. Know before the gate review.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:17,850
Automotive suppliers develop parts through phase gates. Before each gate review, a quality engineer checks by hand that every piece of evidence is in place.

3
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:27,850
Is the PFMEA approved? Does the Control Plan reflect the last design change? Is any high-risk item still open?

4
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:39,850
The evidence already lives in Confluence and Jira. Checking it takes hours, and what gets missed shows up in the review or in a customer audit.

5
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:50,850
GateReady is a Forge app inside the Jira project. One click checks every gate against live Jira and Confluence data.

6
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:56,850
Brake Sensor Housing is 88 percent ready for the Process Design gate.

7
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:11,850
GateReady checks validity, not just existence. The PFMEA is approved and has every required section, but Recommended Actions only says "TBD". So it asks a person to look.

8
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:20,850
EV Battery Bracket uses the same template and sits at the same gate. It is 38 percent ready.

9
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:34,850
The PFMEA is still in progress. The Control Plan is approved, but older than the last design change. The MSA plan is missing, and two high-risk items are open.

10
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:41,850
Every verdict comes with the finding and a link to the evidence.

11
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:51,850
Each gap becomes a Jira work item, assigned to the owner of that evidence, with the finding and links attached.

12
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:56,850
Run it again and nothing is duplicated.

13
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:01,850
The owner updates the Control Plan. Check again.

14
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:10,850
Readiness moves from 38 to 50 percent, and GateReady shows exactly which requirement changed.

15
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:21,850
Every check is kept in the gate's history: when, by whom, and how ready it was. That is the audit trail.

16
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:28,850
Gates, rules, owners and the linked Confluence space are configured per project.

17
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:38,850
Six rule types cover documents and work items: exists, approved, up to date, required sections, all done, and none open.

18
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:45,850
Start from the APQP template, copy another project's plan, or build your own.

19
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:56,850
The Gate Review Agent runs the same deterministic check in conversation and explains each finding.

20
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:11,850
Rules cannot judge whether content is good enough, so the agent reads the document and gives an opinion, clearly marked as advisory. It never changes a verdict.

21
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:18,850
Before it writes anything, it shows what it will create and asks.

22
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:29,850
The review summary is waiting as a Confluence draft: readiness, open gaps, the evidence checklist, and a place for the decision.

23
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:42,850
After the review, record the gate as passed. GateReady saves the date and the readiness at that moment, and moves the project to its next gate.

24
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:54,850
The same six actions are exposed as an MCP server and packaged as a reusable skill, so other agents can prepare gate reviews too.

25
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:00,850
GateReady runs entirely on Atlassian. No data leaves the platform.

26
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:10,850
GateReady. For quality engineers and project managers who need to know before the gate review, not during it.

